et al., 2005), which has myriad applications. For instance, the dry larval biomass can contain 20 %–60 % protein and more than 25 % lipids, which can be captured as valuable animal feed (Gold et al., 2018; Kim et al., 2020; Siegrist et al., 2023) with potential animal health benefits (Huis, 2020). When reared on underutilised biowaste streams, such as food waste, BSFL can be more sustainable than current feed ingredients, such as fishmeal (Smetana et al., 2019). Additionally, marketable substances, such as chitin or antimicrobial proteins, can be extracted from the larvae (Ravi et al., 2020). Finally, the residues from the rearing process (hereafter referred to as ‘frass’) are plant fertilisers (Schmitt and de Vries, 2020). Due to their versatility BSFL become attractive for urban biowaste recycling, which is intrinsically heterogeneous. Such heterogeneous waste often exhibits significant variations in its properties, including its moisture content and its nutrient composition (AbdelShafy and Mansour, 2018). This variability can lead to common challenges in BSFL bioconversion, such as high heat production (Erbland et al., 2021), insufficient frass drying (Lalander et al., 2020) and high NH3 and CO2 emissions (Froonickx et al., 2023; Parodi et al., 2021). Even though these processes are interrelated, they are not studied in combination. Their elucidation is key since they can have dramatic consequences for the efficiency and reliability of the bioconversion. For instance, heat production can influence larval escape, growth and mortality as well as the ability to separate frass and larvae, which are operational problems that undermine the sustained economic and environmental viability of this technology. The bioconversion process has mostly been characterised by monitoring larval mass, biowaste reduction, bioconversion, and survival rates in controlled feeding trials. Because BSFL live in their substrate/frass (i. e. biowaste), the temperature can be significantly different from the ambient temperature. Thus, the importance of substrate/frass temperature monitoring has been recognised recently but it has been monitored by only a few studies (Erbland et al., 2021; Li et al., 2023). In fact, Erbland et al. (2021) prototyped a ventilated incubator (six crates, 0.27
m2 per crate) that tracked mass loss and substrate/frass temperature throughout BSFL bioconversion with chicken feed. The data suggest that microbial activity plays an important role in the rearing of BSFL. Nevertheless, there is a lack of research that attempts to reconstruct a more comprehensive picture of BSFL bioconversion by measuring both insect life history parameters and the abiotic environment by monitoring temperature fluctuations as well as the exchange of water and emissions during the rearing process. From answering fundamental biological questions to designing climate systems for efficient BSFL bioconversion, the simultaneous quantification of gaseous emissions and heat production during the bioconversion is key. Respiration chambers are commonly used to monitor gaseous emissions from living organisms to determine energy and nutrient requirements and collect data for environmental impact assessments of protein production. For instance respiration chambers are used for the assessment of the metabolic activity of plants, soil, or large livestock, such as cattle, however chamber size and the instruments used vary significantly (Albrizio and Steduto, 2003; Carbone et al., 2019; Hammond et al., 2016; Heetkamp et al., 2015). Recent evaluations of BSFL bioconversion have been conducted using dynamic open-circuit respiration chambers, where gas concentrations were continuously measured in ventilated chambers throughout the process (Padmanabha and Streif, 2019; Parodi et al., 2020, 2021). Parodi et al. (2020, 2021) monitored BSFL bioconversion with pig manure and an industrial rearing substrate in an open-circuit respiration chamber
(three rearing crates per chamber, 0.15 m2 per crate or one rearing crate
per chamber, 0.5 m2 per crate). The goal was to track the development of CO2, N2O, CH4, O2 and NH3 gas emissions and to calculate comprehensive nitrogen, carbon, and energy balances to assess the environmental sustainability of BSFL rearing. In contrast, Padmanabha and Streif (2019) designed a low-cost chamber for automated BSFL rearing and growth model development (Padmanabha et al., 2020, 2023). This setup was capable of monitoring CO2 and O2 concentrations, air
temperature, and humidity, as well as the substrate/frass temperature and humidity. However, it failed to capture NH3 emissions, which are crucial indicators of larval protein metabolism and industrially relevant air pollutants (Froonickx et al., 2023). Additionally, only a single crate
(0.12 m2 per crate), smaller than industrial size, was housed in their setup. Consequently, there is a need for an accessible monitoring system that can comprehensively assess the metabolic activity and trends in the BSFL bioconversion processes of different substrates under conditions that resemble practical implementation scenarios while adhering to scientific standards. Few studies have determined gaseous emissions (CO2, CH4, N2O and NH3) from BSFL food waste bioconversion, mainly for environmental impact assessments (Ermolaev et al., 2019; Lindberg et al., 2022; Mertenat et al., 2019; Pang et al., 2020). Large intervals between sampling points complicate the determination of total emissions and neglect common emission dynamics. Additionally, emission samples were drawn from static systems (non-ventilated containers at the time of sampling), in which increasing gas concentrations during measurement could affect instantaneous larval and microbial activities, thereby weakening the estimate. For instance, Guo et al. (2021) measured gaseous emissions from commercial BSFL food waste bioconversion. The sampling was performed at intervals of 4 h but only over a period of 3 days of the whole process, which did not enable the authors to draw conclusions for the whole process. Unlike composting, a 6–7-day BSFL bioconversion process is highly dynamic, and frequent sampling must cover the entire process duration. For instance, CO2 emissions related to larval and microbial activity can peak within hours, while NH3 emissions may surge only 2 days before the harvest of larvae (Coudron et al., 2024; Parodi et al., 2020, 2021). Heterogeneous food waste is a challenging but key substrate in urban biowaste management and sustainable BSFL rearing and, therefore, needs urgent further exploration. The aim of this study was to propose, describe, and validate an industry-relevant bench-scale respiration system and use it for the first time for the high-resolution characterisation of BSFL food waste bioconversion. The system was validated for CO2 recovery, air flow homogeneity, and its ability to capture the bioconversion within and across parallel chambers using food waste in comparison to a reference substrate. The results illustrated that a comprehensive BSFL bioconversion characterisation helps to understand BSFL food waste recycling and to identify areas for further research. Therefore, this study established a tool to improve the bioconversion performance of BSFL with urban biowaste for the production of feed and fertiliser for a circular food system.
2. Material and methods
2.1. Respiration chamber for fly larvae bioconversion
The respiration system included four equally designed respiration chambers that could be individually operated (Supplementary Fig. 1). Fig. 1 depicts the general setup and components of the respiration chamber, including the positions of sensors, whereas Table 1 depicts the key system specifications. The respiration chamber was ventilated through air inlet and outlet ducts for optimal sensor deployment and determination of emission production and water removal from the respiration system. The walls of the chamber were made of polyisocyanurate panels with an aluminium coating (30 mm; Kingspan, Kingscourt, Ireland) to resist humidity and corrosion. For optimal sampling and crate handling, the lid of the chamber was liftable from the top, additionally tightened by rubber bands at the rim to ensure the airtightness of the respiration system. The air inlet and outlet ducts were covered with a plastic mesh (~1 mm) to prevent pests (e.g. insects) from entering the respiration system and disrupting measurements. Unplasticised polyvinyl chloride (UPVC) ducts were connected to the inlet and outlet of the chamber (Supplementary Fig. 2). Cable glands were applied to seal the ports through which the sensor cables passed the duct or
