2.2. Bioconversion and respiration parameters
The BSFL bioconversion process was comprehensively characterised by measuring CO2 (DOL 119, dol-sensors, Aarhus, Denmark), relative humidity, and temperature (DOL 114, dol-sensors) in the inlet and outlet duct (Fig. 1) using commonly used industrial sensors in livestock farming (Supplementary Table 1 for full sensor list with specifications). The exhaust air was further analysed for NH3 (DOL 53, dol-sensors). A fan (San Ace 9LG Series, Sanyo Electric Co., Osaka, Japan) in the outlet duct sucked the air through the hypobaric chamber, establishing a mass flow from inlet to outlet. A perforated plate (polycarbonate) was positioned just after the inlet of the chamber to distribute air homogeneously over the inner chamber cross-section (Supplementary Fig. 2, Table 1). An airflow sensor (UltraLink FTCU100, Lindab, Båstad, Sweden) was placed at the end of the inlet duct next to the chamber inlet to reduce turbulence in the airflow and ensure precise flow measurements required for accurate mass balances. To further mimic industrial BSFL bioconversion, three commonly used rearing crates (60 cm × 40 cm × 19 cm, BSF Breeding Box 190 mm, Beekenkamp Verpakkingen, Maasdijk, Netherlands) were stacked in the chamber, each representing a biological replicate of rearing performance metrics (e.g. larval mass, bioconversion rate, and waste reduction). A scale (FPGA Company,
Tann, Switzerland) consisting of a load cell connected to two aluminium plates (570 mm × 370 mm × 8 mm) as load carriers was placed below the crates to analyse the net mass change of substrate and larvae during the bioconversion. Complementary, three industrial temperature sensors (DOL 12, dol-sensors) per crate recorded the substrate/frass temperature as an indicator of metabolic activity. Each temperature sensor was connected to a weight (50 g) to ensure their submergence and position in the substrate/frass during biowaste bioconversion (corner, intermediate long side, centre; Supplementary Fig. 3). Except for the scale, sensor signals were processed via a controller unit (830 insect monitoring 10′′ L 32RL, SKOV, Roslev, Denmark) and saved in a CSV file using the corresponding software (Farm Online Export 5.0, SKOV). The fan speed was controlled via a pulse-width-modulated (PWM) signal using a single-board computer (Raspberry Pi 4, Raspberry Pi Foundation, Cambridge, UK).
2.3. Experimental design for technical validation
In the absence of larvae and substrate, the respiration system was validated by completing tests on air tightness, air flow homogeneity and CO2 recovery. Airtightness is crucial for avoiding drawing in ambient air, which produces underestimated emission results. In addition, homogenous airflow is the key to ensure balanced heat and water transfer throughout the three crates. CO2 recovery tests are the standard for commercial high-tech respiration chambers for conventional livestock to confirm airtightness, flow rate measurements, and validity of CO2 measurements. Airtightness was additionally confirmed for all chambers according to Heetkamp et al. (2015) by placing soapy water under maximum airflow at each crucial junction (lid, cable glands, and air ducts) and confirming the absence of bubble formation. Airflow homogeneity was determined in one of the chambers by measuring air velocity across the chamber cross-section perpendicular to the airflow at five predefined positions over each crate (Supplementary Table 2) with a handheld hotwire anemometer (425, Testo, Titisee-Neustadt, Germany). The anemometer was positioned in the chamber via sealable ports at a constant airflow and 10 s mean air flow velocities were measured for each position. The CO2 recovery test was completed in all chambers based on Heetkamp et al. (2015) and compared the difference in CO2 mass loss from a commercial gas cylinder (99.8 % CO2) to the CO2 mass loss
