2.4. Experimental design for biological validation and heterogenous food waste assessment
For the biological validation of the respiration system, i.e. the quality of the setup to collect reproducible metabolic and rearing performance metrics in BSFL feeding trials, the variability of measured parameters within and across respirations chambers was assessed using different rearing substrates. Metabolic metrics included substrate/frass temperature, CO2 production, and net mass loss of substrate and larvae, whereas the two latter metrics were only measured on chamber level. Rearing performance metrics were based on manually collected data measured on crate level and included bioconversion rate, substrate reduction, survival rate, individual larval mass, total frass yield, total larval yield and frass moisture content. To this end, special focus was placed on the variability of the measured parameters within and across the respiration chambers (Fig. 2) in these trials. First, the variability within the respiration chambers (or across crates in the same chamber) was assessed to validate the performance of crates as biological replicates. As three substrate/frass temperature sensors were placed in each crate, the substrate/frass temperature differences within the crates were assessed. Second, the variability across the respiration chambers was assessed to validate the performance of the respiration chambers as biological replicates. Parameters reported as contents including the final larval mass were aggregated as averages per chamber, whereas the remaining parameters were aggregated as sums per chamber (e.g. average frass moisture content per chamber and sum of larval yield per chamber). Standard deviations thereof were used as measures of variability. However, to render results more comparable across metrics with different units, the coefficient of variation (standard deviation divided by mean) was used for the variables frass yield, total larval yield, water removal, CO2 production, and mass loss of substrate and larvae. Biological validation was completed in two separate controlled feeding trials, one with food waste and the other with chicken feed used as control. Each trial involved BSFL bioconversion in all four respiration chambers in parallel. For the two trials, one uniform substrate was used across all four chambers to assess the expected baseline variability of the setup.
In a third feeding trial on heterogeneous food waste, the composition of the food waste was varied slightly across the four parallel chambers (Supplementary Table 3) to assess the potential of the setup to capture the resulting changes in metabolic processes during BSFL rearing. To simulate a scenario of variation that closely mirrors practical applications, different levels of the common bulking agent cocopeat (0 %, 2 %, 4 % and 6 % of total wet mass) were added to food waste, whereas each chamber received one bulking agent concentration.
2.5. Substrate composition
Food waste consisting mainly of rice, noodles, and, to a smaller extent, meat and vegetables was collected from a university canteen (Ridge View Residential College, National University of Singapore, Singapore), where it was disposed of within approximately 12 h before collection. Following collection, the food waste was stored at 4 ◦C until use. Before the feeding trial, food waste was ground (Bio Universal Shredder BG2-5,5, voran Maschinen GmbH, Pichl, Austria; see Supplementary Figure 4 for photos). Chicken feed provided by a local distributor was mixed with water to reach a final moisture content of 65 %. Subsequently and before larvae addition, for efficient product separation at harvest, cocopeat was added to the food waste followed by thorough mixing to absorb free water, according to Dortmans et al. (2017), whereas in the trial with heterogeneous substrate across chambers cocopeat was added to induce heterogeneity (Table 2 for cocopeat contents).
2.6. Black soldier fly origin
The BSFL used in the trials originated from a laboratory colony (National University of Singapore) that was maintained for at least 30 generations (Zhang et al., 2024) and initially set up based on wild caught or laboratory cultures. Neonates and larvae of this colony were reared on chicken feed and canteen food waste, respectively. For this study, fly eggs from the colony were placed in an empty container covered with a paper towel for 48 h (Deruytter et al., 2023). Neonates were nursed on chicken feed (62 % water, 9 g of neonates, container: 37 cm × 50 cm × 9 cm). However, for the biological validation with food waste, an external supplier (Ento Industries Pte Ltd., Singapore) provided larvae that had been nursed on a vegetable-based substrate.
2.7. Feeding trials
The three trials were conducted using the described respiration chambers (Table 2 for feeding trial parameters) located in a ventilated laboratory on the National University of Singapore (NUS) campus with a mean relative humidity of 82.8 ± 3.4 % and temperature of 28.5 ± 0.9 ◦C in 2023, based on one year long measurement from the inlet duct of one chamber. At the beginning of the trial, the substrate was brought to room temperature, thoroughly mixed, and sampled for compositional analysis. Each rearing crate was then gradually filled with the substrate (food waste or chicken feed) on a rotational basis to avoid batch effects. Subsequently, an equal number of 5–7 day-old larvae were transferred into each crate. Using two spoons, three grab samples, covering the entire surface and vertical substrate/frass profile, were drawn per crate every second day during the trial and at harvest. Samples taken from the same crate (for day 0 and harvest, substrate/frass was mixed instead) were pooled before moisture content, pH, and individual larval mass analyses, whereas material was returned to the respective crate. At harvest, larvae were separated from frass by sieving, whereas if the substrate was too clumpy for sieving larvae were manually picked, and their mass assessed as rearing performance metrics. The total number of larvae per crate at the beginning and at the end of the trial as well as the larval mass were estimated based on random samples of approximately 100 larvae.
