2.8. Substrate, frass and larval characterisation
BSFL bioconversion inputs and outputs i.e. substrates (one sample per substrate before start of the trial), larvae, and frass (one sample per crate at harvest) were characterised for physicochemical properties (Supplementary Table 4 for detailed methods), such as ash, hydrolysed crude fat, and total nitrogen. Nitrogen-to-protein conversion factors of 6.25 and 4.76 were used for substrate and larvae, respectively (Siegrist et al., 2023). Frasses and substrates were analysed for neutral detergent fibre (NDF), acid detergent fibre (ADF), starch, and glucose. Substrate, frass and larvae were analysed for dry matter (DM) by oven drying at 60 ◦C for at least 3 days or longer until no weight change observed. The pH of the substrate/frass was determined in a 1:5 Milli-Q water suspension using a pH meter (FEP20, Mettler Toledo, Columbus, USA).
2.9. Data post-processing and analyses
Comprehensive BSFL bioconversion process characterisation included sensor-based estimation of CO2 concentration, NH3 concentration, airflow, air temperature, air relative humidity, substrate/frass temperature, net mass loss of substrate and larvae, and manually collected data to estimate rearing performance metrics: bioconversion rate, substrate reduction, and survival rate. Sensor-based metabolic process parameters (except for net mass loss of substrate and larvae) were rounded and normalised via the software FarmOnline (SKOV). The data were automatically transferred to cloud storage for post-processing, visualisation, and statistical analysis in R version 4.1.3 (R Core Team, 2022) using RStudio (Posit team, 2023). Post-processing included the removal of erroneous data when the chambers were opened for sample collection based on air flow and CO2 dynamics. Airflow-based data removal was conducted where differences between two consecutive
timepoints exceeded ± 5 m3 h 1 because during closed operation air flow differences were much lower than this cut-off. Because these airflow changes had lag effects on the CO2 concentrations at the point of measurement (outlet duct), removal of erroneous data had to be extended. This outlier removal was based on the difference between actual and smoothed data points based on local regressions (LOESS), where < 1 % of total data was removed. CO2 emissions were estimated every two minutes based on the difference in CO2 between the ingoing and outgoing air multiplied by the current airflow, considering their specific volume at the current temperature (Supplementary Equation 1). Total water removal during the feeding trial was estimated via an input–output based approach ie. the mass and moisture content of water in the substrate versus in larvae and frass. For a continuous, sensor-based approach of estimating total water removal and substrate/frass dryness, the balance of water entering versus water leaving the chamber via the air was calculated. Briefly, sensor data for temperature and relative humidity were used to calculate the absolute humidity in the ingoing and outgoing air via the respective dew points (Sensirion AG, 2021), specific humidity, and water vapour pressure (Cai, 2019). The absolute water removal per time step and the
total water removal during the trial were determined by factoring in the current airflow rate. The net mass loss of the substrate and larvae in one chamber was calculated based on the difference between the start mass and the sensed mass at each time point during bioconversion. Erroneous mass change data between each measurement (1 min), e.g. due to sampling, were removed (>= 0.01 kg or =< 0.01 kg). The average substrate/frass temperature per crate was estimated based on the mean value from the three substrate/frass sensors as an aggregate measure of substrate/frass heat. Here, the temperatures between rearing days 1 (24 h) and 5 (120 h) were used to characterise the substrate/frass temperature during the window of main activity. The rearing performance metrics, bioconversion rate, substrate reduction, and survival rate, were calculated according to Gold et al. (2020) whereas the values for initial substrate mass, frass, and initial and final larval numbers and masses were used. If not indicated otherwise DM values are stated.
3. Results and discussion
BSFL bioconversion, an emerging technology for biowaste valorisation, still has significant process inefficiencies, particularly when treating heterogeneous biowaste streams with varying compositions, such as urban food waste. Therefore, this study proposes and validates a novel respiration chamber system to investigate the impact of varying substrate compositions on BSFL performance parameters that resemble those of industrial insect farming to develop a more reliable and efficient BSFL-based food waste treatment. For the first time, this study presents highly resolved insights into the metabolic processes related to the bioconversion of food waste with BSFL.
3.1. Technical validation
The respiration system was technically validated by measuring air tightness, airflow homogeneity, and CO2. The results indicate that the chamber performed well in terms of CO2 recovery. In addition, the chamber exhibited some heterogeneities in the airflow velocity. In the CO2 recovery test, 95–105 % of injected CO2 was recorded by the respiration system (Supplementary Table 5). Concerning the airflow homogeneity within the chamber, a trend of higher air velocities in the middle of each crate and in the bottom crate was observed (Supplementary Table 2). However, variabilities of air flow velocities within and across crates were not high (standard deviations: within,
0.03–0.06 m s 1; across, 0.05–0.06 m s 1). One advantage of the presented setup is the possibility of using three stacked industrial-size rearing crates to serve as biological replicates and mimic industrial conditions, in contrast to the open-circuit respiration chamber by Padmanabha and Streif (2019). Yang and Tomberlin (2020) investigated the impact of scale on feeding trials and demonstrated that crate size is crucial for producing data representative of industrial BSFL bioconversion. In contrast, Parodi et al. (2020) had a similar configuration to that of this study; however, the rearing parameters of their three crates were not analysed separately, and airflow velocities over the
