3.2. Biological validation
Biological validation included determining metabolic and rearing performance metrics during controlled feeding trials in the chambers. Comparisons of variability within and between chambers were performed using uniform substrates across the chambers to further validate the applicability of the chamber system for addressing research questions on feeding trials. Within-chamber validation included comparison of rearing performance metrics across three rearing crates in one chamber and the substrate/frass temperature within one crate. The between-chamber validation included a comparison of all measured parameters across the four parallel chambers (Fig. 2). In general, the results indicated that the presented respiration chambers provide rearing conditions that are well-suited for BSFL rearing, while generating representative results. Metabolic and rearing performance metrics in this study were even higher than those reported previously (Erbland et al., 2021; Gold et al., 2018; Manurung et al., 2016; Naser El Deen et al., 2023; Parodi et al., 2020). Survival rates were around 90 %, final larval mass around 60 mg, bioconversion rates 22–28 %, substrate/frass temperatures > 36 ◦C for several days, and
peak CO2 production rates between 20–50 g h 1. Peak substrate/frass
temperatures of up to 50 ◦C at individual spots likely did not provide conditions that are optimal for larvae (Chia et al., 2018) but have been reported previously in BSFL and blow fly larvae systems (Erbland et al., 2021; Slone and Gruner, 2007), and proved that the proposed system could be used to investigate the common issue of substrate/frass overheating. These generally high values for larval mass, survival rate and substrate/frass temperature were expected, given that food waste and chicken feed are among the most nutrient- and energy-dense waste streams fed to the BSFL. In fact, the present study emphasizes the significant potential of food waste to be efficiently recycled via BSFL due to its nutritional quality as reported previously (Naser El Deen et al., 2023). For instance, Manurung et al. (2016) reported a final larval mass of only less than 16 mg for BSFL fed with nutrient-poor rice straw. Notably chicken feed and food waste used for the biological validation had similar starch (food waste: 27 %, chicken feed: 30 %) and protein (food waste: 24 %, chicken feed: 24 %) contents (Supplementary Table 3 for more detailed information on substrate characteristics).
3.2.1. Baseline variability within chambers
Rearing performance metrics generally showed small variability across rearing crates, with the frass moisture content as an exception (Supplementary Tables 6 and 7). With both tested substrates (i.e. chicken feed and food waste), variability within chambers was on average 0.5 ◦C in substrate/frass temperature, < 2 % in bioconversion rate and < 5 mg in final larval mass. Variability in larval survival was lower for food waste (1.6 %) than for chicken feed (7.2 %), which could be due to higher peak substrate/frass temperatures with chicken feed and associated higher larval mortality in some crates. However, for frass moisture content and total frass yield, average variability across crates was larger (5.2 %–9.7 % and coefficients of variation: 17.0 %–19.7 %, respectively). This is likely attributable to recorded airflow heterogeneities (see Section 3.1). Variability of fat and protein content of larvae
within chambers was low with < 1 g 100-1 g 1 except for one chamber
where protein varied by 1.3 g 100-1 g 1 (Supplementary Table 3). In
frass, the nitrogen content varied by only ≤ 0.12 g 100-1 g 1. The higher variability of the frass moisture content might have indirectly increased the variability of the other measured parameters; however, if at all, this effect was low, given the low variability in all other parameters. This low variability suggests that individual crates within one chamber are well suited as biological replicates for rearing performance metrics. In fact, by obtaining three biological replicates, one chamber alone would be sufficient to conduct a feeding trial for a comprehensive study of a specific substrate. Depending on the research question for which chambers will be used, a lower variability in frass moisture might still be crucial. Because variability is likely induced by vertical airflow heterogeneities, regular swapping of crate positions could account for airflow heterogeneities and decrease the frass moisture variability. Additionally, spatial heterogeneities in the substrate/frass temperatures within the crates were assessed. The results from the three temperature sensors in each crate confirmed the importance of using several substrate/frass temperature sensors in each of the industrial-sized crates (Supplementary Figures 3, 5, and 6) for a robust estimation of the average temperature per crate. Hourly average temperatures across sensors differed by more than 3 ◦C within the same crate over several hours. Differences acrosssensors within one crate were as high as 6 ◦C with both substrates. Importantly, at several time points, the temperature trends within a crate developed oppositely, particularly within the first 50 h of the trial (Supplementary Figure 6). Having robust measurements of substrate/frass temperature is crucial because it is the environment in which larvae live and is, therefore, a main factor for the larval development rate (Wang et al., 2023). In fact, as shown below (Fig. 3A), substrate/frass temperatures can differ between substrates by up to 15 ◦C. This is remarkable given that the only external input was ambient air; therefore, the substrate/ frass temperature was mainly driven by the metabolic activity of larvae and microbes (Li et al., 2023). The observed variability emphasizes the need for future studies to measure substrate temperature. Spatial differences in substrate/frass temperatures are expected in BSFL rearing because of larvae roaming in swarms through the substrate/frass (Shishkov et al., 2019), where areas with denser aggregations of metabolically active larvae likely lead to locally higher substrate/frass temperatures (Gligorescu et al., 2019; Li et al., 2023; Slone and Gruner, 2007). Previous studies conducted daily measurements with an infrared camera to capture the entire spatial variability of the substrate/frass temperature (Li et al., 2023), however only the surface temperatures were considered here. With three continuously measuring substrate/ frass temperature sensors per crate, the setup of this study provides a robust estimate. However, further research is necessary to identify the optimal way to capture three-dimensional spatial variability.
3.2.2. Baseline variability across chambers
The variability across the chambers was lower than the previously mentioned variability within the chambers (Fig. 2, Supplementary Table 8). With both substrates, the variability was < 1 % for bioconversion rate, < 0.6 ◦C for substrate/frass temperature, < 3 mg for final larval mass and 2 % for survival rate. Similarly, for both substrates, the variability (coefficient of variation) was < 2.5 % for larval yield, < 2.7 % for frass yield, < 1 % for water removal, and ≤ 1.9 % for net mass loss of substrate and larvae. Likewise, the variability in product composition
was low for fat and protein contents of larvae, varying by 0.55 g 100-1
g 1 and 0.36 g 100-1 g 1, respectively. Total CO2 production differed moderately between chambers in the feeding trial with food waste with a coefficient of variation of 8 %. However, in the trial with chicken feed, variability of CO2 production was exceptionally high at 17 %. This outcome might be due to nonuniform waterlogging in chicken feed across crates, resulting in different microbial activities or other unknown reasons (Bekker et al., 2021). The sensor-based approach for water removal estimation yielded higher average values and higher variability than the input–output-based approach, which requires further investigation. However, discrepancies in the average values
