could be partly explained by the release of metabolic water from the substrate, which was not reflected in the initial moisture content. For instance, in this study, approximately 1.4 kg of metabolic water could have been released based on the nutrient composition of food waste and frass and larvae using common conversion factors for metabolic water release (Mellanby, 1942; Morrison, 1953). Generally, marginal variability across chambers has implications for two possible applications of the respiration system. First, four chambers, each with the same substrate, can be used as replicates to robustly estimate the average total CO2 emissions, time points of the highest CO2 production, substrate/frass temperatures, or bioconversion rates when a specific substrate is converted. Second, the chambers can be used to conduct parallel comparisons of the impacts of different substrates. When the chambers are run in parallel, each with a different substrate, the generally determined low across-chamber variability serves as a reference and sets the bar for the effect size. For example, in trials with uniform food waste or chicken feed across the chambers, the variability in the final larval mass was 1 or 2 mg, respectively. Therefore, when comparing the effects of treatments on larval mass across parallel chambers, differences exceeding the 2 mg baseline may be attributed to a treatment effect beyond the variability introduced by the use of different chambers. A baseline of 2 mg is low considering that treatment effects on the final larval mass are generally more pronounced. For instance, NH3 pretreatments on substrates by Peguero et al. (2023) changed final larval mass by at least 9 mg. In addition to the provision of aggregated and endpoint values, the respiration chamber system enables investigating and comparing temporal metabolic patterns of the bioconversion process (e.g. substrate/ frass and air temperature, relative humidity, gaseous emissions, and net mass loss of substrate and larvae). For instance, CO2 production curves showed consistent temporal patterns (maxima, minima, and slopes) across chambers for each substrate but altered patterns between different substrates (Supplementary Figures 7 and 8). With food waste,
the CO2 production rate rose steeply within the first 24 h of the trial, slowed down until day 2, and peaked at a production rate of approxi
mately 50 g CO2 h 1 between days 3 and 4, after which it declined continuously until harvest, intercepted by a short plateau phase towards the end of day 5. In contrast, with chicken feed, the CO2 production curve was skewed towards the end of the trial, with a slow start, peaking at the end of day 5. Notably the initial larval mass was lower in the chicken feed trial (Table 1) which might have caused a relative delay of the CO2 peak. Across substrate comparisons show that the system is suitable for highlighting and differentiating the effects of a changing substrate composition on the bioconversion. Continuous time-series data with small sampling intervals, as shown in this study, can be valuable for improving process understanding, but comparative analyses of curves are often qualitative. Future research should explore the feasibility of shape-based quantitative analyses of time series, which could help identify and classify recurring metabolic patterns in bioconversion processes, such as further investigating the contribution of substrate/frass microbes versus BSFL to CO2 production (Bekker et al., 2021). Potentially suitable methods for quantitative shape based analyses of biological timeseries have recently been described (Dove et al., 2023).
