larva-1 (coefficient of variation: 23 %). In fact, higher total CO2 production was accompanied by higher average temperatures in the substrate/frass, higher net mass loss of the substrate and larvae, and fastergrowing larvae, especially during the first half of the trial (Fig. 3). In contrast, the bioconversion rate and final larval yield decreased with increasing CO2 production. Likewise, the four food waste mixtures exhibited distinctive patterns of CO2 production over time, whereas for the same food waste mixture, the substrate/frass temperature and CO2 production showed highly similar patterns. Both CO2 production and heat are indicators of metabolic activity; however, periods of strongest CO2 production and larval mass growth aligned only for food waste with 2 % and 4 % cocopeat addition. Increased larval or microbial activity might have been a driver of CO2 and substrate temperature (Bekker et al., 2021; Erbland et al., 2021; Slone and Gruner, 2007). In this study moisture contents differing across food waste mixtures by 3 % might have had an influence on microbial activity and therefore on CO2 and heat generation in the substrate, however Bekker et al. (2021) indicated that rather differences in substrate moisture contents of 10 % lead to decisive changes in microbial activity. Similarly, strong differences in CO2 emissions from BSFL bioconversion were observed by Parodi et al. (2020, 2021) notably by using very distinct inputs: an industrial rearing substrate based on yeast concentrate and starch versus pig manure. The peak CO2 emissions per larva were less than half of those observed in this study. The CO2 production curves exhibited, depending on the substrate, either one pronounced peak (similar to food waste with 4 % fibre addition in this study) on rearing day 5 of the trial or a gentle, plateaushaped maximum (similar to food waste with 0 % fibre addition in this study) from rearing days 5 to day 7, respectively. Total CO2 emissions from pig manure were 40 % lower compared to the industrial rearing substrate, whereas the authors suggested the lower nutritional quality of the latter as an explanation. In this study a nutritional reason for an increased biological activity is unlikely since the common bulking agent, cocopeat, which was used to introduce variation in food waste composition across chambers, had a negligible nutritive value (80 % ADF and 17 % ash content). Additionally, the total amount of food waste per crate did not differ. Notably, past feeding trials frequently used “food waste” as one category when bioconversion parameters or the environmental effects of BSFL rearing on different substrate types were compared (Boakye-Yiadom et al., 2022; Naser El Deen et al., 2023). However, the results of this study suggested that even the bioconversion of nutritionally similar food wastes can lead to process variabilities comparable to those between pig manure and a highly nutritive industrial rearing substrate. In fact, the implications for industrially relevant heat generation and resulting requirements for climate control systems are substantial. Based on the close association between heat and CO2 in livestock systems (Pedersen and Sa ̈llvik, 2002), it can be estimated that during the peak periods in this study, approximately 50 or 20 Watts of heat was generated per crate, depending on the food waste mixture. Extensive heat production requires cooling and therefore energy, which is an environmental but also an economic concern for this waste management technology given that 25 % 56 % of electrical energy consumption in livestock systems is used for cooling. The findings from this study put into question the way substrates are currently categorised for performance assessments and waste recycling. Further research is necessary to identify the substrate characteristics that lead to the observed temperature patterns, as the management of heat and larval growth are key optimisable variables in the recycling of BSFL biowaste (Li et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023). The presented time-series data confirmed the potential of the chamber system to characterise and improve the efficiency of food waste bioconversion processes.
4. Conclusions
This study describes and validates a novel, accessible respiration
chamber system that can be used to holistically investigate metabolic processes in BSFL rearing under conditions that resemble those at the industrial scale. Additionally, this study provides unique, highly resolved insights into the metabolic processes that occur during BSFL bioconversion of food waste. Validation trials confirmed the suitability of the setup to address the research questions from a biological and technical perspective. The low baseline variability within and across chambers indicates that crates and chambers could serve as biological replicates to discriminate the impact of different inputs on the bioconversion process. The present study, using the chamber system, showed for the first time that the addition of a nutritionally negligible bulking agent to food waste could lead to processes with strongly differing temporal metabolic characteristics and products. Shown substrate formulation dependent increases in CO2 emissions are highly relevant for the environmental performance of the BSFL bioconversion. Future work should focus on the technical refinement of the chamber, including the achievement of an accurate estimate of the water balance and the sampling of additional complementary metabolic indicators such as O2 and CH4 to compose full energy and material balances. The presented chamber system should be used to further investigate the physical, chemical, and biological characteristics of rearing substrates that drive CO2 emissions, heat production, and larval growth in BSFL rearing. This will pave the way for better prediction and control of bioconversion processes, which is highly necessary when using heterogeneous biowastes. Additionally, these findings will help to reduce CO2 and NH3 emissions from BSFL bioconversion, thereby improving its environmental performance.
